      "Thinking_Token_Abstract_Screening": 373,
      "Candidate_Token_Abstract_Screening": 123,
      "Input_Token_Abstract_Screening": 1252,
      "Total_Token_Abstract_Screening": 1748
    },
    {
      "Paper_ID": "Liang_2025_Digitaltwinforl_bff42",
      "Year": 2025,
      "Title": "Digital twin for lettuce growth in six climate zones (‘Climate 2050′)",
      "Authors": "Liang, Shu (57233527000); Duc Long, Nguyen Van (60035942400); Parvin, Mst Irin (60035482600); Escribà-Gelonch, Marc (59901563500); Lantin, Stephen (57300990900); Hessel, Volker (7003726937)",
      "Abstract": "This study investigates lettuce growth under extreme environmental conditions by simulating the weather in six climate zones in a plant growth chamber, including Lleida, Adelaide, Paris, San Luis, Singapore, and Fairbanks. The experiment involved weekly exposure to a new city's climate, simulating “non-terrestrial weather stress,” which is also motivated from the vantage point of space plant growth and its process-control limitations. These simulated conditions shed light on ‘Climate 2050’, when Earth will probably have harsher and more fluctuating conditions. For the period investigated, the real temperature changes could be reproduced well and in real-time in the growth chamber, the actual rain fall was mimicked, and the lighting period was adjusted to the real sunshine exposure in the respective city. The virtual move of the lettuce plant from between six climates with their own profile in temperature, lighting time, and water is assumed to create stress beyond the variability of a weather change within a single climate. Machine learning models, including linear regression, random forest regression, and boosted decision tree regression, were employed to predict weekly lettuce biomass and yield. This study successfully demonstrated the application of machine learning algorithms for predicting lettuce growth under the given range of six climate conditions. Among the tested models, random forest regression consistently delivered the most accurate and reliable biomass predictions, achieving an R2 of nearly 99 % and MAPE of 6 % in all scenarios. By introducing tuned correction factors for conditions like drought stress, fertilisation, and mixed soil composition, the accuracy and flexibility of models are enhanced. This research highlights the value of integrating real-time data with machine learning through a digital twin framework, offering a promising direction for climate-resilient agriculture and space-based plant growth systems. © 2025 The Author(s)",
      "DOI_Link": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compag.2025.110880",
      "Link": "https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105012953827&doi=10.1016%2Fj.compag.2025.110880&partnerID=40&md5=5baef03cfcf7fe577f54f2e206ad1ff9",
      "Publisher": "Elsevier B.V.",
      "DOI": "10.1016/j.compag.2025.110880",
      "Conference name": "",
      "Source": "scopus_09-04-2026",
      "Thinking_Token_Abstract_Screening": 7863,
      "Candidate_Token_Abstract_Screening": 229,
      "Input_Token_Abstract_Screening": 1449,
      "Total_Token_Abstract_Screening": 9541
    },
    {
      "Paper_ID": "Ji_2025_Researchondigit_d6cac",
      "Year": 2025,
      "Title": "Research on digital twin of three-dimensional reduced order model of marine composite propeller blades based on multisensory fusion",
      "Authors": "Ji, Xiukun (57465302900); Wang, Jinlong (55675220500); Gong, Yongjun (57710106900); Bao, Yongjie (23134791000); Li, Puze (59968888600)",
      "Abstract": "This research presents a framework for constructing a digital twin structure aimed at evaluating the mechanical state of carbon fibre reinforced plastics (CFRP) propeller blades during water impact. Initially, the mechanical state of CFRP blades under various water impact conditions was assessed through fluid-structure interaction (FSI) simulations. Concurrently, the performance of CFRP blades equipped with strain sensors was designed and tested across different water impact scenarios, successfully realizing the physical system construction of the digital twin. To address the challenges posed by multiple sensors and environmental noise in real systems, a multi-layer stacked denoising autoencoder (SDAE) was employed for sensor signal fusion. The resulting features were subsequently input into a Softmax classifier for the recognition of water impact velocity. Furthermore, the predicted flow velocity served as an input signal to the multi-fidelity surrogate (MFS) algorithm, facilitating the prediction of the mechanical field of the CFRP blade. Lastly, a three-dimensional reduced order model (3DROM) was introduced to minimize the size of the CFRP blade digital twin model and enhance calculation speed while preserving the mechanical properties of each blade layer. Analysis of the results indicates that the accuracy of fused noise sensor signals in identifying water impact velocity reaches approximately 98 %. Although the mechanical field prediction accuracy of CFRP blades decreases with increasing sensor position deviation and flow rate, it remains at an acceptable level of 89 %. MFS predictions, enhanced by the 3DROM, demonstrate a response time that is 11.2 times faster than that of unattenuated MFS predictions. © 2025",
      "DOI_Link": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oceaneng.2025.122071",
      "Link": "https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105009436037&doi=10.1016%2Fj.oceaneng.2025.122071&partnerID=40&md5=add72bc65edef4dc9ff36db89329333a",
      "Publisher": "Elsevier Ltd",
      "DOI": "10.1016/j.oceaneng.2025.122071",
      "Conference name": "",
      "Source": "scopus_09-04-2026",
      "Thinking_Token_Abstract_Screening": 987,
      "Candidate_Token_Abstract_Screening": 340,
      "Input_Token_Abstract_Screening": 1548,
      "Total_Token_Abstract_Screening": 2875
    },
    {
      "Paper_ID": "Cao_2025_Physicsdrivendi_953cc",
      "Year": 2025,
      "Title": "Physics-driven digital twin model and CNN-GRU network for remaining useful life prediction in rolling bearings",
      "Authors": "Cao, Zhuyong (59944907300); Zhou, Jianxing (14009073500); Xiao, Ke (59944621000); Zhou, Yadong (59068574600); Fei, Xiang (58654022100)",
      "Abstract": "In order to enhance the accuracy and adaptability of remaining useful life (RUL) prediction for rolling bearings, this paper proposes a hybrid prediction framework that integrates digital twin (DT) modeling with a CNN-GRU deep learning architecture. First, the discrete wavelet transform is applied to denoise the measured vibration signals, and 13 multi-dimensional health indicators are extracted to characterize the bearing degradation features, whose relevance is further assessed using feature sensitivity and redundancy analysis. Subsequently, a bearing dynamic model and defect evolution model are established based on Hertzian contact theory, and the Snow Goose Algorithm is introduced for dynamic model updating, enabling lifecycle estimation of defect size and generating full-lifecycle simulated vibration signals. In the prediction stage, a CNN-GRU model is constructed, where the CNN convolutional layers extract local spatial features and the GRU units capture long-term dependencies in the time series, thereby enabling deep modeling of the bearing degradation process and RUL prediction. Experiments are conducted on the XJTU-SY bearing accelerated life dataset under three different data scenarios: ‘digital twin data only’, ‘hybrid data’, and ‘measured data only’. The results show that the proposed CNN-GRU model outperforms conventional CNN and GRU models across all error metrics. It effectively addresses the limitations of traditional methods under conditions of limited full-lifecycle bearing data. It provides reliable technical support for the intelligent maintenance and optimization of rolling bearings. The effectiveness and engineering potential of the DT-assisted RUL prediction method are thoroughly validated. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.",
      "DOI_Link": "https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6501/adfcf9",
      "Link": "https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105015048453&doi=10.1088%2F1361-6501%2Fadfcf9&partnerID=40&md5=733520a028fe37a19ed07e7770e6a34f",
      "Publisher": "Institute of Physics",
      "DOI": "10.1088/1361-6501/adfcf9",
